{"text":"I need some advice on a machine learning project I'm working on. So we've developed these models to do artifact detection. Specifically in blood pressure, um a lot of most of the work so far has focused solely on blood pressure. Right now I'm working and we had good results. For blood pressure we did individual pulse level segmentation. And so we created this large data set with a bunch of artifact pulses that were hand annotated. And we tried a lot of different models that use different approaches. The one I'm really focusing on right now is the one that worked the best, which counterintuitively I guess maybe, is a pre-trained image classifier that I fine-tuned on quote-unquote images of the pulses. What I essentially did is like took this like normalization and then convert into a 2D array and treated them as images, fine-tuned the models on those and they did pretty well. They are very accurate and for our use case we really care about like sensitivity and so we were able to get some pretty good results on sensitivity. Now the challenge is now I have to move over to ECG. With ECG instead of doing the individual pulse level segmentation we did 10 second long windows and so we have a chunk. This is 10 seconds ECG. Is this artifact or no? And with that we can like keep or remove those 10 second chunks based on whether or not they're artifact. We have a data set similar size about 10,000 pulses about 7,500 or no around 7,000 is for training the remaining is for test and validation equally split. Now I just did the first two runs with the models and they're they're out in great so far I'm like at 80% accurate 70% sensitivity on the test set about 80 something percent specificity and like maybe 87% AUC. So okay, but not great. The issue I see here is that the I'm not a fan of the like image creation approach. I think it can be improved. Especially because these are pre-trained image models. I almost wonder if like giving actual plots would wouldn't wouldn't be better than giving these like 1D flat images. Like these like squished very like simplified images that just have points. I think that's not really an ideal thing for training the image models. So far they're perform like they're performing okay with the ECG, but I'd really like to see better results. And so I wanted to get your input on what changes you think I can make to the featurization approach or not the featurization, but like the plotting approach. I need something that I can do quickly so I can generate the plots on the fly, not like have to pre-cache all the plots. Ideally something that I can do is like a simple change just for like the experiments I'm running tonight so I have some results to show and then some pointers on other things I could try to improve the performance. The focus really should just be on these 10 second long ECG plots. We can go back to improving the ABP ones at a later date. But I've attached all the code so you can review it and I'll attach screenshots of the results I just got. The green line is from a regular training run. The purple line is from when I have um over sampling turned on. Um yeah. So just go through it, put on your machine learning engineer hat and give me your thoughts on this problem."}